---
import EngineFamilyComparison from "../../../components/studies/EngineFamilyComparison.astro";
import EngineSlotHighlights from "../../../components/studies/EngineSlotHighlights.svelte";
import EngineSlotPlayground from "../../../components/studies/EngineSlotPlayground.svelte";
import EngineSurfacePlayground from "../../../components/studies/EngineSurfacePlayground.svelte";
import FusionChamberConcepts from "../../../components/studies/FusionChamberConcepts.astro";
import IonFamilyProportions from "../../../components/studies/IonFamilyProportions.astro";
import LightIonConcepts from "../../../components/studies/LightIonConcepts.astro";
import PlasmaChamberConcepts from "../../../components/studies/PlasmaChamberConcepts.astro";
import PlasmaVesselConcepts from "../../../components/studies/PlasmaVesselConcepts.astro";
import PlasmaVesselFirstPass from "../../../components/studies/PlasmaVesselFirstPass.astro";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "engine-identities");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<section aria-labelledby="purpose">
		<h2 id="purpose">01 / Recognize the engine before it fires</h2>
		<p>
			Choosing an engine should feel like choosing a different kind of machine.
			Consider inspecting one in the catalog, then fitting it to a small, angled
			side mount: its low-poly body should retain its identity at both scales,
			even with the engine off. Silhouette, proportions and a few clear facets
			must carry that distinction without relying on a label, color or plume.
		</p>
		<p>
			Engines form families, with room for subtypes within each family. Each is
			an architectural choice: its advantages come with costs, and suitability
			for a side mount can differ from suitability for an aft mount. Family
			identity should survive changes in size and capability; a subtype should
			look purpose-built rather than simply ranked above its sibling.
		</p>
		<p>
			The agreed scope includes how characteristics inform those identities:
			continuous or pulsed thrust, acceleration, powered top speed, mass and
			energy demand. These are investigation axes, not assigned engine behaviors
			yet. Light ion, heavy ion, fission and a fourth engine remain the starting
			lineup to test, within a language that can accommodate future subtypes.
		</p>
		<p>
			The tension is between recognizable machinery in a catalog and compact
			forms that remain legible on a crowded ship. We will examine standalone
			bodies and fitted versions together. Plumes and exhaust effects are
			outside this study. The parallel
			<a href="/lab/studies/ship-slot-language">slot language study</a>
			owns mounting cues and fitting interactions; this one owns the installed
			engine's identity.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="exploration">
		<h2 id="exploration">
			02 / Family resemblance and architectural differences
		</h2>
		<h3>02.1 / Shared construction, different proportions</h3>
		<p>
			One visual hypothesis: repeated construction identifies a family, while
			proportions and component count identify its subtype. The ions share a
			longitudinal spine and split outlet; fission uses a compact shielded body;
			a provisional pulse engine uses separated chambers. These are body
			sketches, not accepted technology assignments or simulated performance.
		</p>
		<EngineFamilyComparison />
		<p>
			All four use the same neutral palette. Each appears enlarged in the
			catalog and at the same world scale in the production Fighter hull and
			mount coordinates, once as a side pair and once as an aft pair. Hull
			facets and mount transforms come from shared definitions; the engine
			geometry is a study fixture. Forward is up. No plumes or other equipment
			are shown. This comparison can reveal resemblance, crowding and
			orientation; it cannot establish handling, pulse timing or balance.
			Suggested roles below each body are hypotheses for later flight
			investigation.
		</p>
		<p>
			Review: the owner found 02.1 successful in showing that three distinct
			families, including ion subtypes, can work. The proposed styling is far
			from the intended aesthetic; no engine shape or performance assignment was
			accepted. The next investigation deliberately shifts to faceted surfaces,
			shared lighting and the engine's relationship to the hull.
		</p>
		<h3 id="surface-playground">02.2 / Engines in the ship's light</h3>
		<p>
			Start with light ion in all four Fighter mounts. Try a raised ridge or a
			beveled deck, charcoal or mid-tone bodies, and restrained electric-blue
			accents. Move the sun or rotate the ship: the hull and engine faces use
			the same directional shading, including the angled side engines. Compare
			above-hull and below-hull layering separately for the side and aft pairs.
		</p>
		<EngineSurfacePlayground client:load />
		<p>
			This is a study-local fake-3D lighting model using the production Fighter
			hull and mount coordinates. Engine geometry and material values are
			experiments; the other three bodies are comparison fixtures, not settled
			designs. Below-hull means draw-order occlusion, without cast shadows or
			physical mounting depth. Accents are unlit color inlays, without glow.
			Lighting is not yet integrated into the game. Controls work with keyboard
			and touch; settings are temporary and reset when this page reloads. Plumes
			and handling remain outside this example.
		</p>
		<p>
			Review: the owner accepted dark charcoal as the engine material direction
			and found the faceted fake-3D treatment successful. The playground offered
			too many options for this stage. For the Fighter, side engines belong
			below the hull and aft engines above it, slightly nearer the cockpit. Ion
			blue belongs at the emission end. Engine shape remains open.
		</p>
		<h3 id="light-ion-concepts">02.3 / Six light-ion machines</h3>
		<p>
			Six fixed proposals focus on the light ion alone. Each Fighter carries
			four copies of the same engine, with side bodies below the hull and aft
			bodies above. The aft pair moves four model units toward the cockpit in
			this fixture; that amount is provisional. Hull, side positions, angles,
			scale, charcoal material and upper-left light stay constant.
		</p>
		<LightIonConcepts />
		<p>
			The enlarged body beside each description exposes details hidden by the
			hull. Blue is confined to the outlet or recessed emitter. The expected
			straight ion stream informs the axial shapes, but no plume is drawn. Dish,
			collar, focusing prongs and stepped casing are visual proposals, not new
			mechanics. Shared Fighter geometry and mount transforms are reused;
			layering, aft offset and engine bodies remain study-local.
		</p>
		<p>
			Decision: 02.3 / 01 Needle accelerator is the accepted ion shape
			direction: tapered charcoal casing, axial facets, recessed end and one
			narrow blue emission slit. The owner requested the aft pair slightly
			closer to the cockpit again. The six concepts remain unchanged as the
			reviewed reference.
		</p>
		<h3 id="ion-family">02.4 / One needle, three proportions</h3>
		<p>
			Use the accepted needle unchanged as the medium reference, then compare a
			lighter and heavier sibling. Medium is an exploratory stepping stone; the
			intended lineup still contains light and heavy ion only. All three keep
			the same construction, charcoal material, blue outlet and lighting. Only
			casing proportions change: light is 80% as long and 75% as wide; heavy is
			115% as long and 145% as wide as the reference.
		</p>
		<IonFamilyProportions />
		<p>
			Each ship carries four matching engines. Side engines remain below the
			hull; aft engines stay above and move one additional model unit toward the
			cockpit, five total from the production mount position. This is about two
			to three displayed pixels depending on card width. Standalone bodies share
			a common scale so their proportions can be compared directly. Geometry and
			placement are study fixtures; performance remains unassigned.
		</p>
		<p>
			Decision: 02.4 / 02 Medium is the accepted small/light ion body, and 02.4
			/ 03 Heavy is the accepted heavy ion body. The reduced 01 is not selected;
			medium does not become a third product. Preserve those exact proportions,
			facets and blue outlet details. Ion appearance is settled for this cycle;
			its implementation and performance remain separate work.
		</p>
		<h3 id="fusion-chambers">02.5 / Feed machinery and a pulse chamber</h3>
		<p>
			Fusion's intended character is fluctuating power from repeated fusion
			pulses. Its body should explain two connected assemblies: forward
			machinery feeds a separate reaction chamber, and the release opening
			belongs to that chamber. These three proposals compare a neck, exposed
			braces and an open cup. The same charcoal, light, Fighter mounting
			positions and layering are retained; all three use chartreuse so shape can
			be judged separately.
		</p>
		<FusionChamberConcepts />
		<p>
			In each enlarged engine, the injector is at the top, the connector in the
			middle and the reaction chamber with its release opening at the bottom.
			The three color samples repeat the first body unchanged: reactor green,
			acid chartreuse and sulfur yellow are science-fiction visual associations,
			not a claim about the physical color of radiation. Accent is confined to
			the chamber opening, preserving the distinction from ion blue.
		</p>
		<p>
			Fusion is treated here as the newly proposed family, distinct from the
			existing fission engine; it does not silently replace fission. Pulse
			timing, power balance and plumes are not implemented or demonstrated by
			these static bodies. The question is whether the connected machinery and
			chamber read clearly, especially where the ship covers the side engines.
		</p>
		<p>
			Decision: 02.5 / 02 Braced chamber and acid chartreuse (#c6ff32) are
			accepted for the fusion direction. Preserve the separate injector, paired
			struts, open center and aft reaction chamber. The next refinement tests
			pale struts; their color is not yet accepted.
		</p>
		<h3 id="fusion-pale-struts">02.6 / Pale struts against charcoal</h3>
		<p>
			The selected braced chamber now has white-ish, cool ship-colored struts.
			They retain a light and shaded side under the same sun, while remaining
			paler than the charcoal machinery. Geometry, chartreuse outlet, mounting
			and scale are unchanged; 02.5 preserves the darker-strut reference.
		</p>
		<FusionChamberConcepts paleStruts />
		<p>
			Decision: 02.6 is approved as the fusion engine's visual design. Preserve
			the 02 braced chamber geometry, charcoal injector and reaction chamber,
			paired pale ship-colored struts with directional shading, open center, and
			acid chartreuse (#c6ff32) release opening. Its intended output is pulsed;
			timing, balance and plume design remain unresolved. Accepted is not
			integrated: production implementation is still separate work.
		</p>
		<p>
			The intended four-engine lineup now has settled visual designs for small
			ion (02.4 / 02), heavy ion (02.4 / 03) and fusion (02.6). The fourth
			engine is now being explored as a plasma drive with delayed onset. The
			existing production fission engine's migration or replacement remains an
			integration question, not a fifth entry assumed by this study.
		</p>
		<h3 id="plasma-trial">02.7 / Plasma: contain, build, release</h3>
		<p>
			Plasma is the fourth-engine trial: holding thrust initially produces no
			force, then a strong blast. This rewards anticipating a maneuver and makes
			aft mounting a natural fit; it can make quick corrections harder. That
			delayed onset distinguishes it from fusion's repeated pulses. Whether the
			blast becomes sustained output while held, and what release does during
			buildup, remain open behavior questions.
		</p>
		<p>
			Three body proposals make stored energy visible through a contained core,
			retaining bands and a broad outlet. Each uses charcoal, orange accents and
			the same Fighter lighting and mounts, with side engines below and aft
			engines above. All four mounts are populated to expose fit, not to endorse
			plasma for every position. The color strip repeats the magnetic bottle
			unchanged in orange, amber and yellow.
		</p>
		<PlasmaChamberConcepts />
		<p>
			The intended sequence is
			<strong>hold → buildup without thrust → blast</strong>. Chamber indicators
			could show progress during the delay so the input feels acknowledged; that
			feedback is a proposal, not yet selected. These static bodies show an
			energized appearance, not an animation or a flight trial. Delay duration,
			force, energy cost and cancellation need later investigation. Plumes
			remain outside this study; none is drawn here.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Decision:</strong>
			Amber (#ffb52e) is accepted as the plasma accent. None of these three body
			designs is accepted; they remain here as the first exploration.
		</p>
		<h3 id="plasma-vessels">02.8 / Plasma: industrial pressure assemblies</h3>
		<p>
			The first vessel pass was rejected: the containers looked like toys and
			the coil channels were too long. The revised direction is dangerous
			industrial science fiction: larger pressure vessels, reinforced
			restraints, armored connections and short magnetic exhaust throats. Amber
			(#ffb52e) stays accepted.
		</p>
		<p>
			Six variations compare two twin-cylinder assemblies, two long tanks and
			two stacked-sphere assemblies. Charcoal, shared lighting and Fighter mount
			positions stay fixed. Side engines sit below the hull; aft engines sit
			above it.
		</p>
		<PlasmaVesselConcepts />
		<p>
			Static body studies only: the intended buildup and hot-plasma blast are
			not simulated, and no plume is drawn.
		</p>
		<p class="invitation">
			<strong
				>Which assembly feels like dangerous pressure machinery, and which still
				feels too toy-like?</strong
			>
		</p>
		<details>
			<summary>Rejected first vessel pass</summary>
			<p>
				Preserved for comparison: small containers and overly elongated coil
				channels. None of these three shapes was accepted.
			</p>
			<PlasmaVesselFirstPass />
		</details>
		<h3 id="plasma-simplified">02.9 / Armored containment in broad facets</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>Decision:</strong>
			02.8 / 04 Armored containment is the preferred plasma direction. Its
			bottle body and coil chamber work, but the assembly is far too detailed.
			The next pass keeps those proportions and reduces the construction to
			broad low-poly planes.
		</p>
		<PlasmaVesselConcepts simplified />
		<p>
			The small valve, tank straps, side fittings and body indicator are
			removed. One integrated armor edge, two coarse coil bands and the amber
			outlet remain. The Fighter positions, layering and scale are unchanged.
		</p>
		<p class="invitation">
			<strong
				>Does this retain the pressure-vessel character while fitting the game's
				simpler vector language?</strong
			>
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Approved:</strong>
			All four engine appearances are settled: small ion = 02.4 / 02, heavy ion
			= 02.4 / 03, fusion = 02.6 pale-strut braced chamber, plasma = 02.9
			simplified armored containment. Preserve their shapes, charcoal facets,
			blue/chartreuse/amber accents and material feel. This approves appearance,
			not performance tuning or production integration.
		</p>
		<h3 id="engine-slots">02.10 / Engines inside the ship editor</h3>
		<p>
			The next question is how the approved bodies occupy the accepted editor
			slots. A side engine can sit below the hull in flight but needs a
			complete, recognizable representation when editing. Compare a fitted slot
			illustration against physical-size hull layering, then switch to ship-only
			view.
		</p>
		<EngineSlotPlayground client:load />
		<p>
			This Fighter plan reuses production mount coordinates and slot dimensions,
			plus the exact approved engine SVG definitions above. Slot artwork scale
			is experimental. The slot palette and linked-secondary treatment come from
			the completed slot study; this does not reopen engine appearance.
		</p>
		<p class="invitation">
			<strong
				>Does drawing the complete engine inside its slot make fitting clear
				while preserving its identity on the ship?</strong
			>
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Decision:</strong>
			Shrinking an engine to fit a slot loses its visual character. Engine scale
			must match the ship exactly. In the editor, the full engine must be
			visible above the hull, including side engines. The slot is an attachment
			marker, not a box that contains the artwork.
		</p>
		<h3 id="engine-highlights">02.11 / Full-size engines and selection</h3>
		<p>
			Keep the engines visible at full size over a 70% opaque editor hull. The
			selected item uses soft backlight. Unselected bodies retain their normal
			material and a dark separation edge; slot layers draw in a fixed order:
			side, aft, then hull/Q. The side pair remains linked.
		</p>
		<EngineSlotHighlights client:load />
		<p>
			<strong>Layering decision:</strong>
			Side slots are below aft slots; hull/Q slots are above both. Each slot
			draws its background, then its part and highlight, then its border and
			labels. Selection does not reorder slots.
		</p>
		<p>
			Engine geometry, color, size and mount positions are fixed. The accepted
			slot sizes, frames and selection corners are settled and remain unchanged.
			Slot backgrounds sit behind the engines; borders, selection corners and
			labels draw above them. The extra engine-sized brackets have been removed:
			the selected engine uses the approved soft backlight. Flight layering
			remains available for comparison.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Approved for Fighter:</strong>
			Soft backlight around the selected part, 70% hull opacity, full-size
			engines above the editor hull, and fixed side → aft → hull/Q ordering.
			Slot backgrounds stay behind their parts; borders and labels stay above.
			Slot geometry remains unchanged.
		</p>
		<h3 id="scout-engine-slots">02.12 / Scout engine selector</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>Scout placement decision:</strong>
			All three engines sit below the hull in flight. In both views, each engine
			starts at its slot’s top edge and extends outward at full size. The editor
			keeps engines visible above the hull. The linked-slot connector routes
			above the ship.
		</p>
		<p>
			Apply the same treatment to the Scout's current hull. Slot 04 and its
			engine move three model units aft in this clearance trial; other mounts
			retain production positions. Try all four engines in the linked side pair
			and aft slot, especially the wider fusion and plasma bodies. The Fighter's
			extra aft offset is not applied to the Scout.
		</p>
		<EngineSlotHighlights
			chassis="scout"
			title="Scout engine selector"
			client:load
		/>
		<p class="invitation">
			<strong
				>Does the approved editor treatment stay clear on the Scout's tighter,
				angled mounts?</strong
			>
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Scout approved:</strong>
			Preserve the slot-top engine alignment in both views, all three engines
			beneath the hull in flight, slot 04 three model units farther aft, and
			linkage above the ship.
		</p>
		<h3 id="battleship-engine-slots">02.13 / Battleship engine selector</h3>
		<p>
			The battleship uses the current heavy-cruiser hull and its production
			mounts: a linked side pair and three independent aft slots. Try the four
			accepted engines with the same soft selection backlight, 70% editor hull
			and side → aft → hull/Q ordering. Flight starts with side engines below
			the hull and aft engines above it; battleship placement is still under
			review.
		</p>
		<p>
			The rectangular nose support plate has been removed to restore the tapered
			bow. Side slots and their engines move one model unit inward (roughly four
			displayed pixels) and turn five degrees farther outward. All three aft
			slots and their engines move three model units toward the cockpit in this
			placement trial (roughly 12 displayed pixels).
		</p>
		<EngineSlotHighlights
			chassis="tank"
			title="Battleship engine selector"
			client:load
		/>
		<p class="invitation">
			<strong
				>Do the engines sit naturally on the battleship, especially across its
				three aft slots?</strong
			>
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="next">
		<h2 id="next">03 / Readiness for the player trial</h2>
		<p>
			All four bodies and the Fighter, Scout and battleship fitting treatments
			are approved. Existing loadout physics already supplies mass-dependent
			acceleration, independent engine speed taper, energy use and deterministic
			fixed steps. The integration adds per-mount timers, shared engine
			polygons, editor layering and mount-relative exhaust origins. Gravity and
			friction stay unchanged.
		</p>
		<p>
			Heavy ion’s powered ceiling is 750. The remaining numbers below are the
			implementation’s first balance proposal, authorized for player testing
			rather than claimed final. Peak acceleration equals thrust divided by the
			whole ship’s mass; angled side engines contribute only their forward
			component. Mixed engines keep their own ceilings and never brake overspeed
			gained from gravity.
		</p>
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th>Engine</th>
					<th>Mass</th>
					<th>Peak thrust</th>
					<th>Powered ceiling</th>
					<th>Energy/s held</th>
					<th>Response</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr>
					<td>Small ion</td>
					<td>10</td>
					<td>12,000</td>
					<td>500</td>
					<td>2</td>
					<td>Immediate and steady</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Heavy ion</td>
					<td>20</td>
					<td>20,000</td>
					<td>750</td>
					<td>3</td>
					<td>Immediate and steady</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Fusion</td>
					<td>16</td>
					<td>48,000</td>
					<td>550</td>
					<td>3</td>
					<td>0.18s firing / 0.45s cycle (19,200 mean thrust)</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<td>Plasma</td>
					<td>24</td>
					<td>38,000</td>
					<td>700</td>
					<td>5</td>
					<td>0.6s charge, then sustained</td>
				</tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		<p>
			Fusion starts with a pulse on press; release cuts thrust but does not skip
			recovery. Plasma consumes energy during charging; releasing or losing
			power resets its charge. Each side responds independently and aft engines
			activate when both controls are held. Holding inputs draws the listed
			power throughout charging and pulse recovery. No extra heat or fuel
			subsystem is introduced. Player review must assess pulse steering, delay
			readability and endurance.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section aria-labelledby="integration">
		<h2 id="integration">04 / Integration</h2>
		<p>
			<strong>Implemented locally:</strong>
			The approved bodies, mounts, editor treatments and first-pass engine
			behavior now run in the shared builder and custom flight. Public
			deployment is separate.
		</p>
		<p>
			Promote the accepted 02.4/02 and 02.4/03 ion polygons, 02.6 pale-strut
			fusion and 02.9 plasma into shared Canvas/catalog artwork. Promote the
			final mount offsets and side angles into shared mount construction. Draw
			editor hulls at 70%, with per-slot background → full-size engine and soft
			selected backlight → border/labels, ordered side → aft → hull. Scout
			engines all sit beneath its hull in flight; Fighter and battleship aft
			engines sit above.
		</p>
		<p>
			Extend the Zod part contract before simulation and UI consumers. Keep
			timers on each Simulation, advance them in physical time, and apply energy
			delivery to thrust rather than timer speed. Connect HUD charging/pulse
			feedback and plume emission to actual output. Reset old ship saves as
			requested: version 2 accepts only the four new engines, and the builder
			removes version-1 storage.
		</p>
		<p>
			The canonical integrated trial is <a href="/lab/builder">Lab builder</a>,
			with the same shipyard and flight systems at
			<a href="/builder">Play builder</a>. Custom survival remains unranked;
			legacy stock score profiles are unchanged. Verify timing, cancellation,
			overspeed, deterministic replay, slot order, all mounts and browser
			fitting/flight. Existing plume rendering is reused; new plume aesthetics
			and final balance remain follow-up design work. Local verification and
			public release are recorded separately.
		</p>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
.invitation {
	border-left: 2px solid var(--color-acid-lime);
	padding-left: var(--spacing-md);
}
</style>
